Hi all,

Here is a short summary of where the LVOT side project stands. We measured th=
e left ventricular outflow tract (LVOT) in the 1,423 representative UK Bioban=
k heart meshes released by Ugurlu et al. (PLoS One 2025). Each mesh is the av=
erage end-diastolic heart for one sex, age (46=E2=80=9383) and BMI, so the re=
sults describe typical hearts, not individuals. All 1,423 meshes were process=
ed with no errors.

How the LVOT is measured

The LVOT has no natural lower edge, so every cut is anchored to the aortic an=
nulus (where the aortic valve meets the LV wall in the mesh). Cuts are flat p=
lanes parallel to the annulus, 2 mm below it (main result: a closed ring of w=
all) and 4 mm below it (second result). The meshes have no valve leaflets, so=
 below about 3 mm the mitral side of the LVOT is open and is bridged with a s=
traight line (Figure 1).

[Figure 1. How the LVOT is cut: planes parallel to the aortic annulus, 2 mm a=
nd 4 mm below it. At 4 mm the outline is open on the mitral side (no leaflets=
 in the meshes) and is closed with a straight chord.  (attached: fig1.png)]

1. Size: men's outflow is about 3 mm wider at every age

Comparing men and women of the same age and BMI (622 pairs), the aortic annul=
us and LVOT are 2.6=E2=80=933.0 mm (15=E2=80=9316%) wider in men in every age=
 group. Median LVOT diameter at 2 mm is 18.5 mm in women and 21.4 mm in men. =
The gap does not narrow with age, and it stays at about 6% after indexing to =
LV size, so LV size does not fully explain it (Figure 2).

[Figure 2. Men's annulus and LVOT are about 3 mm wider than women's at every =
age (same-age, same-BMI pairs).  (attached: fig2.png)]

2. Shape: the LVOT is oval, more so in women

The annulus is nearly round (major/minor diameter 1.14 in both sexes). Just b=
elow it the LVOT is clearly oval: 1.47 in women vs 1.40 in men at 2 mm, and 1=
.53 vs 1.43 at 4 mm. Women's LVOT is more elliptical at every age, and this d=
oes not change with age. The short axis runs from the septum to the aorto-mit=
ral side, close to the echo parasternal long-axis line (Figure 3).

[Figure 3. The annulus is nearly round; the LVOT just below it is clearly ova=
l, more so deeper and more so in women.  (attached: fig3.png)]

3. The echo circle underestimates LVOT area by about 30%

Ellipse estimates from two diameters are within 7% of the true outline area. =
The standard echo calculation, a circle from the PLAX diameter, measures the =
short axis and underestimates the area by about 33% in women and 29% in men a=
t 2 mm. The extra 4=E2=80=936 percentage points in women would carry into str=
oke volume and continuity-equation valve area (Figure 4).

[Figure 4. Ellipse formulas get the LVOT area within a few percent; the echo =
circle from the PLAX diameter underestimates it by about 30%, more in women. =
 (attached: fig4.png)]

4. What goes with a more oval LVOT: valve-plane geometry, not wall thickness

We added regional wall thickness (12 segments plus the subaortic septum) and =
the aorto-septal angle, and compared each mesh with others of the same sex, a=
ge and BMI. The strongest link is the aorto-mitral angle: a wider (flatter) c=
linical aorto-mitral angle goes with a more oval LVOT. Across all 998 meshes =
with normal walls, a 1 SD wider angle goes with about 0.4 SD more ellipticity=
, and this holds after adjusting for annulus size and wall thickness. A small=
er aortic annulus also goes with a more oval LVOT. Wall thickness itself show=
s almost no link, and meshes with a normal septum but thicker walls elsewhere=
 are no different (Figure 5).

[Figure 5. Features that go with a more oval LVOT (all values as z-scores for=
 sex, age and BMI). Note: 'Aorto-mitral plane angle' here is the angle betwee=
n the ring normals, i.e. 180=C2=B0 minus the clinical aorto-mitral angle, so =
a negative =CF=81 means a wider clinical angle goes with a more oval LVOT.  (=
attached: fig5.png)]

A quick PubMed review fits this: a wider aorto-mitral angle goes with a large=
r LV and mitral annulus, while a narrower angle goes with older age, a small =
hyperdynamic LV, aortic elongation, and obstructive HCM and SAM. So if LVOT s=
hape were an early HCM marker, our data suggest a rounder LVOT with a narrow =
aorto-mitral angle, not a more oval one. That needs testing in individual sca=
ns.

Main caveats

- End-diastole only: the dataset has no systolic meshes, while clinical LVOT =
measurements are usually mid-systolic.
- No valve leaflets: the LVOT is a closed tube for only 2=E2=80=935 mm; deepe=
r sections use a straight chord.
- Averaged hearts: each mesh averages many people, which shrinks the spread (=
e.g. aorto-mitral angle SD about 2.6=C2=B0 vs about 10=C2=B0 in patients). P-=
values are descriptive.
- Cross-sectional: age differences are between groups, not changes over time.

Possible next steps

- Repeat the key measures (LVOT ellipticity and aorto-mitral angle) in indivi=
dual UK Biobank CMR scans, in systole and diastole.
- Test whether a narrow aorto-mitral angle with a rounder LVOT is more common=
 in HCM or in carriers of HCM variants.

Code, tables and full methods are in the private GitHub repository (ilias-nik=
olakopoulos/lvot-ukb). Happy to go through any of it.

Best wishes,
[Name]

